/*!
  \page example_python_ssc_page Screen-space curvature using CUDA and Python

  \section pyssgl_intro_sec Introduction

  This example computes the screen-space curvature \cite Mellado:2013:SSC of images storing positions and normals,
  as \ref example_cu_ssc_page, but the CUDA kernel is compiled to PTX and launched from Python with PyCUDA.

  \note To use %Ponca from Python without writing CUDA code, see the binding \ref pyponca.

  \subsection pyssgl_intro_sec_dep_subsec Installation and usage

  This example requires CUDA (12 or later) and the following Python packages: `pycuda`, `numpy`, `pillow` and
  `matplotlib`. The target `ssgls_py` compiles the kernel to `ssgls.ptx`, and copies it with the script and the input
  data in the build folder:
  \code{.sh}
  cmake --build build --target ssgls_py
  cd build/examples/python
  python ssgls.py -s 10 -p ./data/ssgls_sample_wc.png -n ./data/ssgls_sample_normal.png
  \endcode

  The script loads the input pictures and computes the curvature for a screen-space neighborhood of radius 10 pixels.
  \image html examples/ssgls_input.png "Screen-space curvature, typical input. Left: world coordinates. Right: remapped normal vectors"

  It generates this picture:
  \image html examples/ssgls_result1.png "Screen-space curvature estimation"

  as well as a trace on the standard output, for instance:
  \code
  Load CUDA kernel ........... DONE in 0.024 seconds.
  Init Input data ............ DONE in 13.390 seconds.
  Init queries ............... DONE in 0.720 seconds.
  Set memory configuration ... DONE in 0.004 seconds.
  Launch kernel .............. DONE in 1.505 seconds.

  ###########Configuration#############
  Image size: 1902 x 911
  NbQueries:  1732722
  Scale:      10
  ################Results################
  Max value:  25.3449363708
  Min value:  -31.8370857239
  #######################################
  \endcode

  \note The preparation of the queries uses a naive and inefficient representation with numpy, which makes the total
  process slow. The computation time on the GPU is given by the last line of the timing trace. No re-sampling is
  performed, so this time is directly related to the scale parameter.

  \section pyssgl_cuda_sec CUDA programming

  The technical details related to the CUDA and Python binding for screen-space curvature estimation are as follows.

  \subsection pyssgl_cuda_mypoint_sec Fitting data structure
  \snippet python/ssgls.cu mypoint

  \subsection pyssgl_cuda_weight_sec Weighting function
  \snippet python/ssgls.cu w_def

  \subsection pyssgl_cuda_fit_sec Fitting primitive
  \snippet python/ssgls.cu fit_def

  \subsection pyssgl_cuda_kernel_sec Kernel
  \snippet python/ssgls.cu kernel

  \subsection pyssgl_cuda_access_sec Memory access
  The input data are formatted with numpy, by dimension (in object space), then by screen-space coordinates:
  \snippet python/ssgls.cu data_acces

  \section pyssgl_python_sec Python script

  \include python/ssgls.py
 */
